"<div><div class=\" response-message\"></div></div><div><div class=\" response-message\"><h2>FSM Property Validation</h2>\n<p>✅ Validation complete</p>\n<p>This analysis models the <strong>User Authentication Flow</strong> as a Finite State Machine (FSM) and provides a formal validation report based on the requested properties.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Part 1: State Identification</h3>\n<h4>1. Logged Out</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> The default state where no user session exists.</li>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> Initial</li>\n<li><strong>Invariants:</strong> No valid session token exists in the client or server-side session store.</li>\n<li><strong>Entry Conditions:</strong> Application launch, explicit logout action, or redirection from a terminal error state.</li>\n<li><strong>Exit Conditions:</strong> User submits primary credentials (username/password).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2. Authenticating (Primary)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> A transient state where the system validates primary credentials against the identity provider.</li>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> Normal (Transient)</li>\n<li><strong>Invariants:</strong> Credentials have been received; the system is awaiting a database/LDAP response.</li>\n<li><strong>Entry Conditions:</strong> <code>submit_credentials</code> event from <em>Logged Out</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Exit Conditions:</strong> <code>auth_success</code> (no MFA), <code>auth_success</code> (MFA required), <code>auth_failed</code>, or <code>account_locked</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3. MFA Pending</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Primary authentication succeeded, but the system requires a second factor (OTP, Biometric, etc.).</li>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> Normal</li>\n<li><strong>Invariants:</strong> Primary identity is verified; session is &quot;partially authenticated&quot; but lacks access permissions.</li>\n<li><strong>Entry Conditions:</strong> <code>auth_success</code> event where <code>mfa_enabled == true</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Exit Conditions:</strong> User submits MFA code, requests a code resend, or session times out.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4. MFA Validating</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> A transient state where the system verifies the provided MFA token.</li>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> Normal (Transient)</li>\n<li><strong>Invariants:</strong> MFA token received; system is awaiting verification response.</li>\n<li><strong>Entry Conditions:</strong> <code>submit_mfa_code</code> event from <em>MFA Pending</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Exit Conditions:</strong> <code>mfa_success</code>, <code>mfa_failed</code>, or <code>mfa_attempts_exhausted</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5. Logged In</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> The user is fully authenticated and authorized to access protected resources.</li>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> Normal (Stable)</li>\n<li><strong>Invariants:</strong> Valid, non-expired session token exists; user permissions are loaded.</li>\n<li><strong>Entry Conditions:</strong> <code>auth_success</code> (no MFA) or <code>mfa_success</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Exit Conditions:</strong> User clicks logout, session expires (idle timeout), or administrative session revocation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6. Password Reset Required</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> Authentication succeeded, but the account policy forces a password change (e.g., first-time login or expired password).</li>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> Normal</li>\n<li><strong>Invariants:</strong> Identity verified; access to the main application is restricted until the password is updated.</li>\n<li><strong>Entry Conditions:</strong> <code>auth_success</code> or <code>mfa_success</code> where <code>password_expired == true</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Exit Conditions:</strong> Successful password update or user cancels (returning to <em>Logged Out</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7. Account Locked</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> The account is temporarily or permanently disabled due to security policy violations.</li>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> Error</li>\n<li><strong>Invariants:</strong> User ID is flagged as &quot;locked&quot;; all authentication attempts for this ID are rejected immediately.</li>\n<li><strong>Entry Conditions:</strong> <code>max_failed_attempts_reached</code> from <em>Authenticating</em> or <em>MFA Validating</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Exit Conditions:</strong> Administrative unlock or cooldown period expiration.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>8. Session Expired</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Description:</strong> A previously valid session has become invalid due to inactivity or absolute timeout.</li>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> Error / Terminal (Transient)</li>\n<li><strong>Invariants:</strong> Session token exists but is rejected by the server.</li>\n<li><strong>Entry Conditions:</strong> <code>timeout_event</code> triggered from <em>Logged In</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Exit Conditions:</strong> Automatic or manual redirection to <em>Logged Out</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>Part 2: Validation Report</h3>\n<h4>1. Determinism</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Status:</strong> ✅ <strong>Pass</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Explanation:</strong> Each state transition is triggered by a specific, mutually exclusive event (e.g., a credential check results in either <code>success</code>, <code>fail</code>, or <code>lockout</code>). There are no ambiguous transitions where one event leads to two different states simultaneously.</li>\n<li><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Ensure that the logic for &quot;MFA Required&quot; vs. &quot;Password Reset Required&quot; is prioritized (e.g., MFA should always come before Password Reset to ensure identity before sensitive changes).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2. Completeness</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Status:</strong> ❌ <strong>Fail</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Explanation:</strong> The model does not explicitly handle &quot;System Errors&quot; (e.g., Database Down) in the transient states (<em>Authenticating</em> or <em>MFA Validating</em>). If the database is unreachable, the FSM might hang in a transient state.</li>\n<li><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Add a global <code>system_error</code> event for all states that transitions the user to a &quot;Service Unavailable&quot; state or back to <em>Logged Out</em> with an error message.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3. Reachability</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Status:</strong> ✅ <strong>Pass</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Explanation:</strong> All states are reachable. <em>Logged In</em> is reached via the auth/MFA path; <em>Account Locked</em> is reached via the failure path; <em>Password Reset Required</em> is reached via the policy-check path.</li>\n<li><strong>Recommendation:</strong> None.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4. Liveness</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Status:</strong> ✅ <strong>Pass</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Explanation:</strong> There are no &quot;Sink&quot; states. Even <em>Account Locked</em> has an exit condition (admin unlock/timeout). <em>Logged In</em> can always transition to <em>Logged Out</em> or <em>Session Expired</em>. The FSM is cyclic, which is appropriate for an authentication system.</li>\n<li><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Ensure the &quot;Cooldown&quot; timer for <em>Account Locked</em> is handled by an external system clock to trigger the transition back to <em>Logged Out</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5. Safety</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Status:</strong> ✅ <strong>Pass</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Explanation:</strong> There is no direct path from <em>Logged Out</em> to <em>Logged In</em> that bypasses the <em>Authenticating</em> or <em>MFA Pending</em> states. The invariants for <em>Logged In</em> (valid token) cannot be met without passing through the validation states.</li>\n<li><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Implement &quot;Guard Conditions&quot; on the <code>auth_success</code> transition to double-check that MFA was not bypassed if the user profile requires it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6. Minimality</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Status:</strong> ❌ <strong>Fail</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Explanation:</strong> <em>Authenticating</em> and <em>MFA Validating</em> are functionally very similar (both are transient verification states). While they check different factors, they could potentially be modeled as a single &quot;Verifying Credentials&quot; state with a parameter for the &quot;Factor Type.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Recommendation:</strong> If the backend logic for verifying a password and an MFA code uses the same service/timeout logic, merge them into a single <strong>Verifying</strong> state to simplify the machine. However, if the security requirements differ (e.g., different timeout lengths), keep them separate.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h3>Summary of Recommendations</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Add Error Handling:</strong> Introduce a <code>System Error</code> state to handle database or network failures during transient states.</li>\n<li><strong>Define Timeouts:</strong> Explicitly define transitions for user inactivity in the <em>MFA Pending</em> and <em>Password Reset Required</em> states to prevent &quot;hanging&quot; sessions.</li>\n<li><strong>Refine Transitions:</strong> Ensure the transition from <em>Logged In</em> to <em>Session Expired</em> clears all sensitive data from the client-side state.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></div><div><div class=\"spinner-border\" role=\"status\"><span class=\"sr-only\">Loading...</span></div></div>"